WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.720
There's a lot going on in Bumblebee, even
for longtime Transformers fans.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.440
If your brain was left as boggled as the Autobot's
memory cells after fighting Blitzwing, never

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:09.940
fear.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.760
Let us un-confuse you, and break down the
meaning of the end of Bumblebee.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.200
We won't deceive you — there's spoilers
ahead.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.339
Bumblebee buzzed into theaters to bring moviegoers
a new kind of Transformers film — one focused

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.780
more on characters than screen-shaking action
sequences.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:31.060
The origin story brings back the titular Autobot,
and also introduces the spunky 18-year-old

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.280
Charlie, who discovers him in a scrapyard.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.060
"You sound like a lil bumblebee."

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.400
Bumblebee's concluding act pushes its protagonists
to be quick on their feet, bold with their

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.520
hearts, and unflinching in their choices.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.620
All that's fairly easy for Bee, who proves
willing to selflessly drown himself in a flood

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.200
if it means taking the Decepticon Shatter
with him, and saving humanity.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:57.120
Charlie shows her bravery by diving into the
waters to rescue Bee after the waters overtake

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:57.620
him.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.560
The move is more than just a rescue it also
represents Charlie reconnecting with her past

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.700
as a state champion diver, an activity she'd
rejected after her father's death.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.920
But Charlie's biggest moment of courage was
her personal sacrifice letting Bumblebee go

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.680
instead of keeping a watchful eye over him
herself.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.100
Not only did this demonstrate Charlie's selflessness,
it also set her apart from other protagonists

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.920
in the six-movie-long Transformers series,
who rarely left the sides of their Transformers.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:30.740
Charlie is no action hero, nor is she a child
of destiny she's just a typical teenager,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.060
whose life doesn't really have room for cosmic-scale
conflict.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.720
But it's not like she couldn't team up with
her old Bug again if she can help save the

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.680
world once, she can do it twice, right?

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.520
"Like this"

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:46.400
"Not bad!"

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.740
On a secluded hill overlooking the Golden
Gate Bridge, Bee and Charlie share a tearful

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.360
goodbye after the final battle.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.780
Bee prepares to head to San Francisco, while
Charlie stresses her newfound need to stay

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.180
behind in Brighton Falls with her loved ones.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:05.939
Bumblebee then scans a yellow 1977 Camaro,
sparking some jealousy from Charlie, and then

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.960
rides onto the Golden Gate Bridge alongside
a red Freightliner semi-truck.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:15.230
The truck turns out to be the movie's Generation-1
version of Optimus Prime, while Bee's sweet

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:19.840
Chevy form is the same one he assumes from
the start in 2007's Transformers.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.500
All this seems to create a direct link between
the end of Bumblebee and the start of the

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.640
first Transformers.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:30.280
It's sort of a sweet moment but the more you
think about the timeline of the movie series,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.620
the less sense it makes.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:33.760
"You serious?"

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.930
The Transformers movie timeline is a total
mess of continuity, with every entry in the

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:44.220
series seeming to somehow contradict the one
that came before it.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:49.550
In Michael Bay's first movie, it's explained
that the Autobots landed on Earth in 2007,

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:54.180
motivated to show up by a need to find the
AllSpark — an ancient all-powerful artifact.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:59.540
When Bumblebee learns that Sam Witwicky has
a map that leads to the AllSpark, he contacts

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:04.300
Optimus Prime, who then touches down on Earth
for the first time with three other Autobots.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:08.760
The Bumblebee movie basically tosses that
piece of lore into the trash by having Bee

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:13.640
meet Prime on Earth in 1987, 20 years earlier,
on the Golden Gate Bridge.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.200
The lore is further jumbled by the movie's
mid-credits scene, which features Bumblebee

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.260
and Optimus Prime meeting in a forest, looking
up at the sky, and spotting several Autobots

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.010
careening toward Earth to begin building their
new base — something that just hasn't happened

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.040
by the time the original movie begins.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:35.220
But while this change stomps all over established
series canon, it also opens up room for more

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.400
prequel movies.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.920
If the future films are as good as Bumblebee,
we'll call it a fair trade.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.300
The human antagonist of Bumblebee is Agent
Jack Burns, played by none other than John

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:45.800
Cena.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:51.140
Burns harbors a serious grudge against Bumblebee
after the Autobot crash lands on Earth and

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:56.220
fights a destructive battle against the Decepticon
Blitzwing at the base of Sector 7, the secret

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.320
agency that oversees alien activity.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.519
The cataclysmic battle doesn't just leave
Bumblebee without his voicebox — it also

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.340
gives Burns a nasty scar.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.500
It's not difficult to see why the military
man is quick to team up with the Decepticons

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.680
Shatter and Dropkick when they claim Bumblebee
is an extraterrestrial criminal.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:17.040
Naturally, it turns out that the Decepticons
are nothing but a pair of lying robo-bullies,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.400
who couldn't care less about humankind.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.600
"They literally call themselves Decepticons.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.700
That doesn’t set off any red flags?"

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.500
Burns experiences a change of heart when Bumblebee
saves him from dying in a helicopter crash

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.960
during the movie's final fight.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:35.040
Realizing he's misjudged the robot, Burns
ultimately acknowledges Bumblebee as a soldier

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.420
and a hero, giving him a farewell salute while
Bee pumps his fist in the air, Breakfast Club-style.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.360
Burns buys Bee and Charlie time to escape
before the rest of Sector 7's soldiers show

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:45.860
up.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.640
It's an act of kindness that leaves Burns
open to play a sympathetic, or even heroic

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.400
role in a potential future movie.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.580
Bumblebee leaves plenty of room open for a
sequel.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.240
One mid-credits scene serves as a springboard
for more Autobot adventures, while another

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.730
grants Charlie some closure and some freedom
in the form of a working red convertible.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:09.260
The arrangement leaves this one particular
story feeling wrapped up, but the minds behind

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.320
the series haven't been shy about discussing
what might come next.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:18.520
Transformers series producer Lorenzo Di Bonaventura
has described Bumblebee as being not a full

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.260
reboot, but rather a new direction for the
franchise.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.020
As he said to Entertainment Weekly,

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.500
"If Bee's successful, we can have a Bee 2,
no doubt.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.780
We have a good sense of where a second movie
would go."

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.060
There are plenty of potential paths to choose
from when thinking about the future of Transformers.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:38.310
The movie could be the beginning of its own
franchise, or the start of a series of spin-offs

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.040
focused on other Autobots.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:44.560
If the film is successful enough, Paramount
might greenlight that Optimus Prime solo movie

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.710
people have been chattering about, or put
into development the Cyberton-set animated

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.500
sequel director Travis Knight has said he's
keen on.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:55.990
The studio could take the series years into
the past or decades into future to tell all

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:57.100
sorts of new stories.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.070
In the words of Di Bonaventura,

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.660
"I think we've got an abundance of choices.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:05.860
It's really more about narrowing them down
than anything else, and deciding which one

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:07.740
we think is the strongest one to go with next."

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.520
Whichever direction the Transformers franchise
goes from here, we hope it keeps this new

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.320
commitment to character and quality.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.120
After five movies' worth of sometimes nonsensical
Michael Bayhem, the series has made what's

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.190
being called its best movie yet.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:24.460
As long as the filmmakers keep that up, we'll
be there to ride along.

